<p>In the wake of <a href="https://www.yahoo.com/news/us/articles/faa-orders-national-ground-stop-211429098.html">last Friday’s ground stoppage</a>, the <em>New York Times</em> pointed me to <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/06/us/faa-floppy-disks-atc-2028.html?searchResultPosition=1">this article</a> (that should be a gift link) from just a week earlier about outdated computer technology still in use in air traffic control. I was struck by one of the terms used in the article.</p>
<p>The article is titled “Just 2 More Years Until We’re Done With Floppy Disks, F.A.A. Says,” and it includes an explanation of what those mysterious “floppy disks” are for its younger readers.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Most adults barely remember floppy disks, even if they are old enough to have used them. And the idea that such antiquated technology is still used for important jobs two decades into the 21st century seems laughable.</p>
<p>They were originally produced in the early 1970s at an eight-inch diameter, they shrank to 5¼ inches and then 3½ inches.</p>
<p>The original disk was indeed “floppy,” with a soft plastic case. Later versions that became popular in the mid-80s had harder plastic shells, and didn’t flop anymore, but the name stuck.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><img alt="Different sizes of floppy disk" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261117-Different%20sizes%20of%20floppy%20disk.jpg" title="Different sizes of floppy disk" width="100%"/></p>
<p class="caption">Image from <a href="https://www.tomshardware.com/pc-components/storage/the-floppy-disk-patent-was-granted-today-in-1972-when-80kb-took-up-8-inches-and-were-really-floppy">Tom’s Hardware</a>.</p>
<p>There’s an Apple angle to this. When I bought my first Mac in the spring of 1985, system software, the programs (“apps” hadn’t been invented yet), and my document files were all stored on the 3½-inch medium described above.</p>
<p>But what prompted me to write this was the strong memory that Apple was adamant that these were not “floppy disks.” Apple felt that that term was appropriate for the old-fashioned 5¼-inch disks used in those terribly antiquated IBM PCs,<sup id="fnref:ii"><a href="#fn:ii" rel="footnote">1</a></sup> but should never be used for the sleek, sturdy, and hard-shelled media used in Macs. Apple insisted on calling the 3½-inch variety “diskettes.”</p>
<p>The argument was that the stiff plastic shell made them not floppy.<sup id="fnref:sex"><a href="#fn:sex" rel="footnote">2</a></sup> The actual magnetic disk inside the shell <em>was</em> still floppy, but you were never supposed to see that.</p>
<p>As I recall, the Mac community of the time was pretty good about sticking to Apple’s preferred terminology. I still think of floppy disks as being the 5¼-inch variety, but apparently I’m in the minority. As time went by, everything that wasn’t a hard disk became floppy.</p>
